<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bawa Jan Gwarzo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1771">1771</a>–<a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Bawa of Gobir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bawa Jan Gwarzo was sarkin, or king, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> in present-day northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>. A Muslim ruler who tolerated older religious practices, he invited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> to his court and granted concessions to his community. Under his successors relations broke down, and in 1804 Usman dan Fodio declared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> that destroyed Gobir's power.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bawa was born around 1715 into the royal family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a>. His father, Sarkin Gobir Babari, had become ruler after murdering his predecessor and waged war on several neighbouring states, among them the ally Zamfara, whose capital he destroyed. Bawa's bravery in battle earned him the name Jan Gwarzo, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> name reserved for the bravest warriors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Bawa continued his father's campaign for regional dominance, leading expeditions across Katsina, Yauri, Agadez and parts of Zamfara and subduing Maradi and Birnin Karfi. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a> he extracted a tribute recorded as five thousand female slaves and five thousand domestic slaves, sending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etsu-nupe">Etsu Nupe</a> one hundred horses in return. The power <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> gained under him made it the only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> state to refuse tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu Empire</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>, a reformer from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> town of Degel, was then preaching a return to strict observance of Islamic law. During the Id al-Kabir festival of 1788-89, Bawa, then about 75, invited him to the Id prayers at Magami. Tradition holds that the king first meant to kill him, but recognising Usman's growing popularity, he instead agreed to his five requests, which concerned the freedom to preach, the treatment of Muslims and prisoners, and taxation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At those prayers all of Bawa's scholars left him to join Usman's following of about a thousand, and Bawa is remembered for pointing to the Shaikh and saying that the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> after him would be no more than local heads of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alkalawa">Alkalawa</a>. Late in his reign he was defeated by Sarkin Katsina Agwaragi in a battle whose bitterness, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> chroniclers wrote, beggars description, and in which his son was killed. Bawa died forty days later, unable to bear the loss.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bawa Jan Gwarzo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its rulers were Muslim but tolerated older practices, which the Fulani scholar Usman dan Fodio, once tutor at the court of <b>Bawa Jan Gwarzo</b>, condemned in his preaching.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gobir">Kingdom of Gobir</a></li></ul></section>
